  TechniqueSection(
    title: "Why it matters",
    body:
        "The Swiss army triplet is the fastest way to play flammed triplets, because the lead hand plays two notes in a row and never has to cross. It is the secret behind many impossible-looking fills.",
  ),
  TechniqueSection(
    title: "How to play it",
    body:
        "lR R L: a flam (left grace, right main), a right tap, a left tap, as an eighth-note triplet. Same lead every group; line 2 alternates it.",
  ),
  TechniqueSection(
    title: "Practice tips",
    body:
        "Think \"flam-a-dee\": the first two notes belong to one hand. Keep the left tap at the height of the right tap. Line 5 pairs it with the flam accent so you feel the difference, line 8 doubles the speed.",
  ),
  TechniqueSection(
    title: "Where you hear it",
    body:
        "Drum corps, Scottish and Basel pipe-band drumming, and fusion drummers who want a flammed triplet fill at tempos where the flam accent falls apart.",
  ),
];
